Add lifecycle hooks: twelve events, shell handlers only

JOY_HOOKS=1 makes Joy read <home>/hooks.json and run the user's own shell
commands on twelve events — PreToolUse, PostToolUse, PostToolUseFailure,
PermissionRequest, SessionStart, SessionEnd, Stop, StopFailure, SubagentStart,
SubagentStop, PreCompact, PostCompact. Event names match Claude Code and codex,
so a config copied from either does not need translating.

Two places fire them, deliberately: tool events live in ToolRegistry::execute
(the single choke point, where schema validation already happens) and lifecycle
events live in the app-server, the only layer that knows when a session starts
or a turn ends. PermissionRequest is the odd one: it fires in run_command's
handler, because "should we ask the human" is only known there.

Contract: the payload arrives on stdin as JSON; exit 2 is the only exit code
that blocks (reason from stdout's `reason` or stderr); any other non-zero code
is a non-blocking error that says so on stderr, because a mistyped path must not
disable every tool; a JSON stdout can rewrite tool input, rewrite the result, add
context, or answer a PermissionRequest on the user's behalf. A rewritten input is
re-validated — a hook can break it too, and the error says the hook did it rather
than letting the model think it wrote bad arguments.

Timeouts split two ways (hermes' rule): policy events fail closed, observation
events fail open — a gate that cannot answer in time must not wave things
through, while a stuck observer must not affect what already happened.

A hooks.json changed while running is not executed, with the reason printed:
swapping a live policy hook is the kind of silent change that should stop and be
seen (codex's trusted_hash idea, minus the approval UI we do not have).

Stop's block re-runs the turn once. That is the ancestor of the goal loop
(goal/set) without its round cap, judge or human-authority boundary — building
the general mechanism twice would be worse than having only one of them for now.

Shell handlers only and Unix-first are both deliberate and both in limitations:
want HTTP? curl. Want an LLM to judge? call one. Windows needs a real
executable, and the CI Windows job skips these tests rather than pretending.

13 new tests (config parsing incl. junk entries, exit-2 vs exit-1, decision JSON,
the timeout split, matchers, the changed-file refusal, and a registry-level pair
showing a blocked call and a rewritten result) plus one eval that blocks a tool
call end to end. Docs: configuration, limitations, internals 6, .env.example,
CHANGELOG — EN + zh.
